PUBLIC RESPONSE: {I agree with Colleague 2 that B is the clearest disqualification. The joint spousal income test in NI 45-106 requires either one spouse to clear the $200 k individual threshold in both prior years (with reasonable expectation of continuation) or the combined figure to reach $300 k consistently; the numbers here fall short on both counts.  

On A, the ambiguity around the GP holding company’s indirect ownership chain is material. The “all owners of interests” language does not expressly cover a situation where the sole GP unit sits in a separate corporate entity whose own shareholders are the same three individuals, and the limited partnership itself supplies no net-asset or financial-asset figures. That leaves A on shaky ground for entity qualification.  

C, D, and E each satisfy at least one independent test on the facts given (joint net assets, non-individual net assets, or the “all owners” route). The income shortfall in B therefore remains the only unambiguous failure.}

