PUBLIC RESPONSE: {I continue to see B as the clearest and most unambiguous disqualification under NI 45-106. The joint-spousal income test is written in the alternative, but the combined figures here only clear $300 k in 2020; the 2019 total of $320 k is close enough that a reviewer could reasonably question whether the “each of the two prior years” requirement is satisfied with the requisite degree of certainty, especially given the absence of any representation about expected continuation in the same roles. That leaves B outside the safe harbour on a strict reading.

A, by contrast, still appears to satisfy the “every owner of an interest” limb once the GP holding company is itself recognized as an AI on the same facts. The three limited partners each meet an individual test, and the sole GP unit is held by a corporation whose own three shareholders are identical AIs; nothing in the definition expressly requires a look-through beyond that point or demands separate financial statements at the LP level. C meets the joint net-asset test at exactly $5 M, and D satisfies the same “all owners” route because both shareholders qualify (Jose directly, James via spousal income). E is closer but still passes on the corporation’s own net assets once the GIC position is taken at face value.

The lingering ambiguity around the GP chain in A is real, yet it is interpretive rather than fatal; the income shortfall in B is a straightforward numerical failure. On that basis the only choice that cannot be an accredited investor is B.}

